# Mineral and Energy Resources of India

> Under India's fields and seabed lie the ores, coal seams, oil pools,
> and energy flows that power its industry. This lesson classifies these
> resources, locates the chief belts, and weighs conventional against
> renewable sources.

## 1. Reading the Subsurface Wealth

[definition]
A **mineral** is a naturally occurring substance with a definite
chemical composition that can be mined for profit; an **energy
resource** is any material or natural flow used to generate power.
[/definition]

@diagram tree id="resource-families" layout="tree" aria="Classification of mineral and energy resources"
  "Mineral and energy resources" -> "Metallic minerals"
  "Mineral and energy resources" -> "Non-metallic minerals"
  "Mineral and energy resources" -> "Conventional energy"
  "Mineral and energy resources" -> "Non-conventional energy"
  "Metallic minerals" -> "Ferrous: iron ore and manganese"
  "Metallic minerals" -> "Non-ferrous: bauxite, copper, gold"
  "Non-metallic minerals" -> "Mica, limestone, gypsum"
  "Conventional energy" -> "Coal, petroleum, natural gas"
  "Conventional energy" -> "Hydro and nuclear power"
  "Non-conventional energy" -> "Solar, wind, tidal, geothermal, biomass"
@/diagram

[tip]
**Ferrous** minerals contain iron and give the steel industry its
backbone; **non-ferrous** minerals such as bauxite and copper supply
lighter metals and electrical wiring.
[/tip]

## 2. Iron Ore and Manganese: The Ferrous Backbone

Iron occurs as magnetite (the finest ore, up to about 70 percent
iron), haematite (50-60 percent iron and the most used), limonite,
and siderite.

| Belt | Region | Notable centres |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Odisha-Jharkhand | Eastern plateau | Badampahar, Mayurbhanj, Singhbhum |
| Durg-Bastar-Chandrapur | Chhattisgarh and Maharashtra | Bailadila hills, ore railed to Visakhapatnam for export |
| Ballari-Chitradurga | Karnataka | Kudremukh deposits, mining halted in 2005 for ecological reasons |
| Maharashtra-Goa | Western coast | Ratnagiri and North Goa |

- Odisha is India's largest iron ore producer, followed by
  Chhattisgarh and Karnataka.
- **Manganese**, a ferro-alloy that hardens steel, is mined chiefly
  in Odisha, Karnataka, and Madhya Pradesh.

[important]
Bailadila's high-grade haematite travels by rail to Visakhapatnam and
sails out as export cargo — a neat example of a resource belt, a rail
corridor, and a port forming one economic chain.
[/important]

## 3. Non-Ferrous and Non-Metallic Minerals

| Mineral | Uses | Chief areas |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bauxite | Ore of the light, corrosion-proof metal aluminium | Odisha (Kalahandi, Koraput), Gujarat, Amarkantak plateau |
| Copper | Wiring and alloys | Khetri (Rajasthan), Balaghat (Madhya Pradesh), Singhbhum (Jharkhand) |
| Mica | Electrical insulation in the electronics industry | Koderma belt (Jharkhand), Nellore (Andhra Pradesh) |
| Limestone | Cement making and flux in smelting | Widespread in Rajasthan, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat |
| Gold | Jewellery and finance | Hutti (Karnataka); the Kolar mines closed in 2001 |
| Diamond | Gem cutting | Panna (Madhya Pradesh) |

[example]
India imports most of its copper and gold but is nearly
self-sufficient in iron ore, bauxite, and mica — a pattern that
shapes both its export basket and its import bill.
[/example]

## 4. Coal: The Gondwana Gift

- About 98 percent of India's coal reserves lie in **Gondwana** rocks
  of the Damodar, Son, Godavari, and Mahanadi valleys, mostly
  metallurgical bituminous coal formed some 200 million years ago.
- **Jharia** in Jharkhand holds the richest coking coal; other great
  fields are Bokaro, Raniganj (West Bengal, mined since 1774),
  Talcher, and Korba.
- **Tertiary** coals of Meghalaya, Assam, and Arunachal are young,
  high in sulphur, and fit mainly for power; **lignite** is dug at
  Neyveli in Tamil Nadu.

## 5. Petroleum, Natural Gas, and Electricity

| Source | Leading sites | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Petroleum | Digboi (oldest refinery, 1901), Mumbai High offshore field, Ankleshwar and Kalol in Gujarat, Krishna-Godavari basin | Jamnagar hosts one of the world's largest refinery complexes |
| Natural gas | Mumbai High, Bassein, Krishna-Godavari basin | Carried by the Hazira-Vijaipur-Jagdishpur pipeline; fertiliser feedstock |
| Thermal power | Coal-fired stations sited near mines | Produce the bulk of India's electricity |
| Hydro power | Bhakra (Sutlej), Hirakud (Mahanadi), Nagarjuna Sagar (Krishna), Tehri (Bhagirathi), India's highest dam | Clean but monsoon-dependent |
| Nuclear | Tarapur, Rawatbhata, Kalpakkam, Kakrapar, Narora, Kaiga, Kudankulam | Uranium from Jaduguda; thorium from Kerala's monazite sands |

