[warning]
Coal-fired plants emit ash and greenhouse gases, so India is shifting
capacity toward solar and wind while fitting supercritical boilers
and flue-gas scrubbers to clean up the coal generation it keeps.
[/warning]

## 6. Renewable Energy: Solar, Wind, and Beyond

- **Solar**: the Bhadla park in Rajasthan is among the world's
  largest; the National Solar Mission (2010) and the target of about
  500 GW of non-fossil capacity by 2030 drive rooftop and park
  projects.
- **Wind**: Tamil Nadu (Muppandal), Gujarat, and Maharashtra lead,
  helped by passes along the Western Ghats that funnel strong coastal
  winds inland.
- **Biomass and biogas**: gobar gas plants turn farm waste into
  cooking fuel and leave slurry that serves as manure.
- **Tidal and geothermal potential**: the Gulf of Kachchh and the
  Sundarbans for tides; hot springs at Manikaran (Himachal Pradesh)
  and the Puga valley (Ladakh) for geothermal heat.

[info]
Rapid deployment has made India one of the world's largest markets
for renewable capacity additions, and solar tariffs have fallen by
more than three-fourths in a decade.
[/info]

## 7. Conservation: Stretching Every Unit

- Recycle scrap metal — recycled aluminium consumes far less energy
  than smelting fresh metal from bauxite.
- Use low-grade ores and better technology so leaner deposits become
  viable instead of being abandoned as waste.
- Adopt energy-efficient appliances (BEE star labels), LED lighting,
  and public transport to cut demand at the source.
- Mine under environmental clearance with reclamation and safety
  laws, so future generations keep their share — the essence of
  **sustainable development**.

[example]
The UJALA scheme distributed hundreds of millions of LED bulbs,
saving kilowatt-hours at national scale — proof that conservation is
the cheapest new power plant a country can build.
[/example]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Resource | Leading areas | Key fact |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Iron ore | Odisha, Chhattisgarh, Karnataka | Magnetite runs up to about 70 percent iron |
| Bauxite | Odisha and Gujarat | Ore of aluminium; smelting needs cheap power |
| Coal | Jharia, Bokaro, Raniganj, Talcher | Gondwana coking coal of the Damodar valley |
| Petroleum | Mumbai High, Digboi, Ankleshwar | Crude refined at Jamnagar, Mathura, Barauni |
| Nuclear | Tarapur to Kudankulam | Thorium reserves from Kerala's monazite sands |
| Solar | Bhadla, Rajasthan | National Solar Mission, 2010 |
| Wind | Muppandal, Tamil Nadu | West-coast passes funnel strong winds |
